  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nKind of a typical mid-summer large scale upper level flow regime \nacross NOAM...consisting of western Conus centered upper level \nridge/heat dome and broad northeast NOAM centered troughing. \nMichigan of course remains right under the ridge/trough interface, \nfeaturing primarily west to northwest flow aloft. Large area of \nlower lakes centered high pressure dominating the surface conditions \nacross much of the Great Lakes, bringing with it dry and seasonably \nmild weather to the Northwoods. Southeast dropping cold front well \nto our north in northern Ontario, kicking off a few showers and \nthunderstorms along and just a bit ahead of it.\n\nUpper level flow regime described above essentially remains locked \nin place for the entirety of this forecast period. While this will \nlikely prevent any widespread wet weather as best Gulf moisture \nplume remains detached to our south, passing southeast diving waves \nwill drum up occasional shower and storm chances through the period.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nTemperature trends and addressing those periodic chances or showers \nand storms.\n\nDetails: \n\nAfter some seasonably chilly temperatures tonight, Friday is \nshaping up to be a just about perfect summer day...with near normal \nhighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s under plenty of sunshine. \nTemperatures continue to slowly warm heading into the weekend, with \nwidespread highs in the 80s by Sunday. Weak shortwave trough and its \nattendant surface reflection expected to work into the area \nSaturday, with an eventual completed detachment of mid level support \nlikely allowing its surface component to \"wash out\" on Sunday. While \nlarge scale forcing is weak at best, diurnally-driven instability \nsupport and some modest pre-frontal moisture pooling will bring the \nthreat for at least a few showers/storms Saturday into Saturday \nnight. Not currently anticipating any high impact weather with these \nstorms given such limited deep layer support.\n\nPretty decent mid-range guidance agreement that next southeast \ndiving and likely a bit more robust shortwave visits our area Sunday \nnight into early next week. Could see a bit more robust convective \nactivity with this wave given what looks to be better low and mid \nlevel wind fields. Current SPC graphics suggest best severe \npotential will slice west and south of the Northwoods, with \nconceptual models also agreeing with this idea. Will of course need \nto look out for some isolated areas of heavier rain, especially \nwhere those more moist soil conditions reside (parts of northwest \nlower Michigan). Plenty of time to work out these details in the \ncoming days. Current trends support a relatively uneventful period \nof weather heading into the middle of next week. Airmass source \nregion continues to look to be mostly from Canada heading through \nnext week, with no direct tap to western heat dome. This should keep \nour temperatures well within the range of what is considered normal \nheading through the last week of July.",
